<description>
Carve an alternating background checkerboard through the interior of each solid colored rectangle.

Input structure:
- The background is the most common color.
- Every non-background object is a monochrome 4-connected filled rectangle.
- Each rectangle is at least 3x3, so it has a border and a nonempty interior.

Rule:
- Preserve each rectangle border unchanged.
- Within each rectangle interior, replace cells whose row-plus-column parity is
  even relative to the interior's top-left cell with the background color.
- Leave the other interior cells and all background cells unchanged.

Key insight:
- The output is not moving the rectangles; it applies the same top-left-anchored
  checkerboard mask independently inside every solid rectangular object.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "solid monochrome foreground rectangles on the background",
  "transform": "preserve each rectangle border; inside each rectangle, recolor cells at even parity from the interior top-left to background and leave the alternating cells colored"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 13-19 rows x 15-19 cols
  background: color 0 (black); must be the most common color [observed]
  rectangles:
    count: 1-4
    shape: solid filled axis-aligned rectangles, each a single non-bg color
    dimensions: each side 5-10 cells (minimum 3x3 required by solver)
    colors: each rectangle has a distinct non-bg color (1-9)
    placement: no bounding-box overlap; pairwise gap >= 2 in at least one axis [observed]
    may touch grid border [observed]
    bg fraction: 30-70% of total grid area [observed]
  transform:
    border: 1-cell-wide border of each rectangle preserved unchanged
    interior: cells where (row + col) parity == 0 relative to interior top-left become bg
    remaining interior cells keep original rectangle color (checkerboard pattern)

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct (input-first); transform is deterministic and non-ambiguous
  steps:
    1. Pick grid dimensions H in [13,19], W in [15,19]
    2. Pick num_rects in [1,4]; pick that many distinct non-bg colors
    3. For each rectangle pick width in [5,10] and height in [5,10]
    4. Place rectangles on the grid with no bounding-box overlap (gap >= 1 between any pair)
    5. Fill remaining cells with bg color 0
    6. Output is derived by applying the checkerboard rule to each rectangle interior
```
